\ Exact signed-wide gate position arithmetic, alongside the native walker.
\ A gate index and a gate coordinate are both signed-wide decimal
\ records. The gap 0 does not exist. Crossing gate zero uses the gap
\ owned by the side from which the traversal proceeds:
\    -1 -> 0 uses interval(-1);  0 -> +1 uses interval(+1)
\    +1 -> 0 uses interval(+1);  0 -> -1 uses interval(-1).
\
\ All four signs and every coordinate change use signed wide numbers;
\ no gate index or position is converted to an ordinary CAL number.
\ The interval LENGTH remains an ordinary number in 42..963.
\ The position commits only after a valid canonical interval arrives.
\ This reference traversal is budgeted to 1024 gaps and still invokes
\ a full exact sauce per gap, so it is not suitable for far-away days.
\ The native year/cutlet walkers do NOT yet use this module.
\ CAL compiler execution has not verified this code.

A wide gate position has
  a wide signed day called index,
  a wide signed day called coordinate,
  a number called reached.

A wide gate movement has
  a wide signed day called old index,
  a wide signed day called next index,
  a wide signed day called gap index,
  a number called direction,
  a number called succeeded.

The current extended gate is a wide gate position.
The wide gate movement is a wide gate movement.

To initialize extended gate traversal:
  Put "0" into a wide zero magnitude.
  Set the current extended gate's index from the wide zero magnitude given 0.
  Put "15055671" into a wide foundation magnitude.
  Set the current extended gate's coordinate from the wide foundation magnitude given -1.
  Put 1 into the current extended gate's reached.
  Begin bounded gate walking.

\ No costly sauce work is performed when preparing a move.
To prepare a wide forward gate movement:
  Put 1 into the wide gate movement's direction.
  Put the current extended gate's index into the wide gate movement's old index.
  Put the wide gate movement's old index into the wide gate movement's next index.
  Put "1" into a wide step magnitude.
  Set a wide step day from the wide step magnitude given 1.
  Add the wide step day to the wide gate movement's next index.
  Put the wide gate movement's next index into the wide gate movement's gap index.
  If the wide gate movement's old index's polarity is less than 0,
    put the wide gate movement's old index into the wide gate movement's gap index.

To prepare a wide backward gate movement:
  Put -1 into the wide gate movement's direction.
  Put the current extended gate's index into the wide gate movement's old index.
  Put the wide gate movement's old index into the wide gate movement's next index.
  Put "1" into a wide step magnitude.
  Set a wide step day from the wide step magnitude given 1.
  Subtract the wide step day from the wide gate movement's next index.
  Put the wide gate movement's next index into the wide gate movement's gap index.
  If the wide gate movement's old index's polarity is greater than 0,
    put the wide gate movement's old index into the wide gate movement's gap index.

\ The interval source must have a canonical gap, not index zero.
\ A blocked movement leaves BOTH index and coordinate unchanged.
To commit the prepared wide gate movement:
  Put 0 into the wide gate movement's succeeded.
  Request another bounded gate step.
  If the gate step budget's exhausted is 1, exit.
  Calculate extended gate spacing for the wide gate movement's gap index.
  Apply a measured wide gate gap given the gate inquiry's interval length.

\ This pure movement commit helper is separately testable with known
\ canonical gaps, without recalculating the costly 46-drop sauce.
\ Reject a malformed length before changing either public coordinate.
To apply a measured wide gate gap given a number:
  Put 0 into the wide gate movement's succeeded.
  If the number is less than 42, exit.
  If the number is greater than 963, exit.
  Convert the number to a string.
  Put the string into a wide gap magnitude.
  Set a wide gap displacement from the wide gap magnitude given the wide gate movement's direction.
  Add the wide gap displacement to the current extended gate's coordinate.
  Put the wide gate movement's next index into the current extended gate's index.
  Put 1 into the wide gate movement's succeeded.

To move to the next positive wide gate:
  Prepare a wide forward gate movement.
  Commit the prepared wide gate movement.

To move to the next negative wide gate:
  Prepare a wide backward gate movement.
  Commit the prepared wide gate movement.

\ A caller that requests more than 1024 gaps fails immediately.
\ For a reachable index, every intermediate position remains wide.
To locate a wide gate given a wide signed day:
  Initialize extended gate traversal.
  Put 0 into the current extended gate's reached.
  Put "1024" into a maximum wide gate steps.
  If the maximum wide gate steps is numerically smaller than the wide signed day's absolute quantity, exit.
  Loop.
    If the current extended gate's index's polarity is the wide signed day's polarity,
      if the current extended gate's index's absolute quantity is the wide signed day's absolute quantity,
        put 1 into the current extended gate's reached; break.
    Compare the current extended gate's index with the wide signed day.
    If the signed day precedence result is 1, move to the next positive wide gate.
    Compare the wide signed day with the current extended gate's index.
    If the signed day precedence result is 1, move to the next negative wide gate.
    If the wide gate movement's succeeded is not 1, break.
  Repeat.
